PR #2539  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Misconfigured performance counters silently return constant values rather than trapping, causing security monitoring that depends on counter data to fail silently with no exception to detect the condition.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/cpufunc.h:129-132  (macros) rdcycle, rdtime, rdinstret, rdhpmcounter
      rdhpmcounter(n) performs an unconditional CSR read of hpmcounter##n with zero validation that the corresponding mhpmevent register was ever configured. Per RISC-V privileged spec section 3.1.11, reading an hpmcounter CSR whose mhpmevent is clear returns 0 silently — no trap. rdcycle/rdtime are architecturally always-on and safe. rdinstret requires IR bit in scounteren for U-mode access and could return 0 silently if that bit is clear. The kernel provides no wrapper that validates event-selector configuration before reading HPM counters, making every rdhpmcounter() call site a potential silent-zero return.
  [OK] sys/riscv/include/cpu.h:105-110  get_cyclecount
      get_cyclecount() wraps rdcycle() which reads the cycle CSR. Per RISC-V unprivileged spec section 2.2, the cycle counter is always-on and cannot be disabled — it is architecturally guaranteed to count regardless of mcounteren/scounteren/mhpmevent settings. This function is safe because the underlying CSR has no failure mode that returns a constant zero. The concern is architectural rather than functional: this function establishes the pattern of unconditional CSR-counter reads, which is only safe for cycle/time but is mechanically identical to the unsafe rdhpmcounter() pattern.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_riscv.c:214-216  vmmops_vcpu_init
      guest_scounteren is initialised to HCOUNTEREN_CY | HCOUNTEREN_TM only — explicitly excludes HCOUNTEREN_IR (bit 2, instret) and all HPM counter enable bits (bits 3-31). Per RISC-V privileged spec section 3.1.11, when scounteren has bit X clear, an attempt to read the corresponding counter in VS-mode returns 0 silently (no illegal-instruction trap). A guest OS reading instret or any hpmcounter receives a constant zero with no exception to signal the misconfiguration. This is a direct instantiation of the vulnerability: the hypervisor disables counters but provides no trap mechanism for the guest to detect the disablement, so guest security-monitoring code (e.g., side-channel detection via cache-miss spikes) operates on bogus zero data.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_riscv.c:634-635  vmmops_run
      The explicit TODO comment 'should we trap rdcycle / rdtime?' is a self-documenting acknowledgement that the developers recognise counter reads may need trapping but have not implemented it. hcounteren is written with only CY+TM bits set, leaving IR and all HPM bits (3-31) clear. Per the RISC-V hypervisor spec, when hcounteren bit X is clear, a VS-mode read of the corresponding counter traps with an illegal-instruction exception — notably different from scounteren's silent-zero behaviour. However, the trap handler in vmm_riscv.c does not distinguish counter-access traps from genuine illegal instructions, so the hypervisor cannot provide the guest with differentiated information about why the trap occurred. The TODO confirms this is a known gap, not an oversight.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_switch.S:69-82  vmm_switch (VM entry path)
      The csrrw at line 75 atomically swaps guest_scounteren into the hardware scounteren CSR. The assembly itself is correct as a world-switch mechanism — it properly saves the displaced host scounteren and loads the guest value. The risk is that this assembly is the enforcement point for the VMM's counter-access policy, and it blindly loads whatever value is in hypctx->guest_scounteren with no validation. If guest_scounteren were corrupted (e.g., by a bug in VMM init or restore paths leading to HPM bits being inadvertently set when the host hasn't configured mhpmevent), the guest would see non-zero counter values whose provenance is unknown. Currently safeguarded by the fact that vmmops_vcpu_init explicitly sets only CY+TM, but this invariant is not checked at the enforcement point.
  [OK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_switch.S:158-174  vmm_switch (VM exit path)
      VM-exit path correctly restores host scounteren via csrrw at line 167 and saves the guest's scounteren value back to hypctx->guest_scounteren at line 173. The save-back is architecturally benign because VS-mode cannot write scounteren (it is an HS-mode CSR; sscounteren is a separate CSR read in VS-mode). The guest's scounteren value after VM exit is guaranteed to be identical to the value loaded at VM entry. The world-switch is functionally correct — the vulnerability is not in the switching mechanism but in the chosen guest_scounteren value (only CY+TM) set at init time in vmmops_vcpu_init.
  [OK] sys/riscv/include/encoding.h:1030-1058  (CSR definitions) hpmcounter3-hpmcounter31
      These are DECLARE_CSR macro invocations defining inline accessors for hpmcounter3 through hpmcounter31 (CSR numbers 0xC03-0xC1F). These are passive infrastructure definitions — the DECLARE_CSR guard (#ifdef at line 1023) controls whether they expand to static inline functions. They are not vulnerability sites in themselves; they are the building blocks that rdhpmcounter() uses. The structural problem (definitions exist for counter reads but no code validates event-selector configuration) is in the usage pattern at cpufunc.h:132, not in these definitions.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/encoding.h:1139-1167  (CSR definitions) mhpmevent3-mhpmevent31
      DECLARE_CSR macros define accessors for mhpmevent3 through mhpmevent31 (CSR numbers 0x323-0x33F) — the event-selector registers that must be configured before the corresponding hpmcounter returns meaningful data. These definitions exist in the header, but no kernel code anywhere in the tree writes to these CSRs. Per RISC-V privileged spec section 3.1.15, reset state of mhpmevent registers is UNSPECIFIED for bits 0-6 (event selector) and all remaining bits. If hardware resets mhpmevent to 0 (no event selected), the corresponding hpmcounter reads 0 forever. The kernel cannot distinguish 'counter genuinely 0' from 'counter never configured', and the definitions exist without any corresponding configuration code.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/encoding.h:1062-1075  (CSR definitions) mcounteren, scounteren
      DECLARE_CSR(scounteren, CSR_SCOUNTEREN) at line 1062 and DECLARE_CSR(mcounteren, CSR_MCOUNTEREN) at line 1075 define accessors for the counter-enable registers. mcounteren (0x306) controls which counters are accessible from S-mode — if a bit is clear, an S-mode read of the corresponding counter traps with an illegal-instruction exception. The only runtime write to a counter-enable register in the tree is csr_write(hcounteren, ...) at vmm_riscv.c:635. No kernel startup code reads or validates mcounteren (set by firmware at boot) or writes scounteren for the host. If firmware leaves mcounteren zeroed, all S-mode counter reads trap. If mcounteren is correctly set by firmware, this is safe, but the kernel has no defense against misconfigured firmware.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-isa-manual/pr-2539/

  [RISK] sys/dev/hwpmc/hwpmc_riscv.h:33-47  (stub definitions) RISCV_PMC_CAPS, pmc_md_riscv_pmc
      RISCV_PMC_CAPS advertises PMC_CAP_READ, PMC_CAP_WRITE, PMC_CAP_INTERRUPT, and several other capabilities that are not actually implemented. The pmc_md_riscv_pmc struct defines a pm_riscv_evsel field for event-selector storage, but no hwpmc_riscv.c implementation file exists anywhere in the source tree. This is a forward-looking stub that claims capabilities it cannot deliver. Any PMC consumer (userspace or kernel) that queries these capabilities and attempts to use the advertised features (e.g., configure an event selector via PMC_CAP_WRITE then read the counter via PMC_CAP_READ) would hit the silent-zero problem: the write path doesn't exist to configure mhpmevent, and the read path would return hpmcounter values from unconfigured counters.
  [OK] sys/riscv/include/pmc_mdep.h:30-59  (prototype) pmc_riscv_initialize
      The comment at line 31-32 explicitly states 'On the RISC-V platform we don't support any PMCs yet.' Prototypes for pmc_riscv_initialize() and pmc_riscv_finalize() are declared but never defined — grep confirms no implementation exists. This is an architectural acknowledgement that the MD PMC layer is entirely absent. While this means no PMC functionality is available, the explicit documentation of the gap is honest. The risk is deferred to whenever these functions are implemented: the implementation must validate counter event-selector configuration before exposing counter values to consumers, or it will replicate the silent-zero vulnerability.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:366-371  do_trap_supervisor
      SCAUSE_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION handling unconditionally panics the kernel after dumping registers. The panic message formats stval and sepc but makes no attempt to distinguish the cause of the illegal-instruction trap. Per RISC-V privileged spec, an S-mode access to an M-mode-only CSR (e.g., mhpmevent from S-mode) or a counter CSR disabled by mcounteren triggers an illegal-instruction exception. If future kernel PMU code attempts to access a counter whose mcounteren bit is clear (due to misconfigured firmware), the system panics with no recovery path, no diagnostic indicating it was a counter-access trap, and no way to signal the misconfiguration to the operator beyond the raw register dump.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:434-449  do_trap_user
      User-mode illegal-instruction handler delivers SIGILL/ILL_ILLTRP for all non-FPU cases. A user-space PMU tool reading a disabled hpmcounter (e.g., via the rdpmc instruction or direct CSR-read inline asm when scounteren bit is clear) receives SIGILL with si_code ILL_ILLTRP — identical to the signal delivered for a genuinely invalid instruction encoding. There is no POSIX-defined mechanism to distinguish 'counter is disabled' from 'instruction is illegal', but the kernel makes no attempt to differentiate these cases internally (e.g., by inspecting stval for the CSR address). A userspace tool cannot programmatically detect that its counter read failed due to misconfiguration rather than a code bug.
